  "slug": "lipid-associated-a1a3",
  "summary": "Simulations and in vivo assays now resolve how proteins distort bilayers to bind membranes and assemble into oligomers.",
  "synthesis": "The four preprints converge on how proteins engage lipid bilayers through membrane perturbation and interface-specific contacts. Molecular dynamics simulations of 13 plant lipid droplet proteins, combined with in vivo tobacco pollen tube assays, identify distinct membrane binding modes, including binding via a hydrophobic face. Coarse-grained simulations of ten channels and transporters in POPC bilayers reveal membrane defects at oligomeric interfaces that may drive lipid-mediated oligomerization. A new Python package, MembraneCurvature, built on MDAnalysis, computes membrane curvature from simulation trajectories. Biophysical characterization of the yeast Osh4 ALPS-like motif, using CD, fluorescence, ITC, XRD and MD simulations, shows preferential binding to anionic TGN membranes. The new elements are the comparison of binding modes across lipid droplet proteins and the framing of oligomeric interfaces in terms of membrane defects.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-11-28"
}
